'''Chun Ho Carlos Cheung''' ({{lang-zh|t=張雋浩|cy=Jēung Jeun-houh}}) is a New Zealand politician. He was elected as a Member of the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]] for [[Mount Roskill (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Roskill]], representing the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], in the [[2023 New Zealand general election]]. Cheung has worked as a medical researcher and owned two property management companies.

== Career ==
Cheung was born in Hong Kong and moved to New Zealand as a teenager. He attended [[Auckland Grammar School]] and the [[University of Auckland]], where he studied science and eventually graduated with a PhD in biological science. His 2013 thesis was on diabetes-induced cardiac disease, and his doctoral advisors were [[Garth Cooper]] and Shaoping Zhang.<ref>{{Cite thesis |last=Cheung |first=Carlos |title=Acquired copper imbalance in diabetes-induced cardiac disease: Molecular mechanisms and reversibility |date=2013 |degree=PhD |publisher=ResearchSpace@Auckland, University of Auckland |hdl=2292/20334 |url=http://hdl.handle.net/2292/20334}}</ref> He worked as a medical researcher but decided to leave research due to difficulty finding funding.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |date=22 October 2023 |title=New National MP on switching from science to property management |language=en |website=1 News |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/10/22/new-national-mp-on-switching-from-science-to-property-management/ |access-date=22 October 2023}}</ref> He set up Lifestyle Property Management in 2014 and later started a second company, Enfield Property Management.<ref name=":3" /><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=New Zealand National Party |date=28 March 2023 |title=Dr Carlos Cheung Selected As National's Candidate In Mt Roskill |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO2303/S00216/dr-carlos-cheung-selected-as-nationals-candidate-in-mt-roskill.htm |access-date=14 October 2023 |website=www.scoop.co.nz}}</ref>

== Political career ==
{{NZ parlbox header|align=left}}
{{NZ parlbox|term=54th|start={{NZ election link year|2023}}|end=present|list=48|party=New Zealand National Party|electorate={{NZ electorate link|Mount Roskill}}}}
{{End}}Cheung was announced as National's candidate for [[Mount Roskill (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Roskill]] in March 2023<ref name=":0" /> and was also a National Party list candidate, ranked 48th. On election night, Cheung won the electorate with an 1,564-vote lead over incumbent [[Michael Wood (New Zealand politician)|Michael Wood]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Mt Roskill - Official Result |url=https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-25.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=25 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123104118/https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/electorate-details-25.html |archive-date=23 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=15 October 2023 |title=Red seats turn blue? National ahead in Labour's Auckland strongholds |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/auckland-electorate-election-results-red-seats-turn-blue-national-ahead-in-labour-strongholds/D7EASK4PPVCRRA7GXSIROU2R4E/ |access-date=14 October 2023 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref> Mount Roskill had been regarded as a safe seat for Labour, with Wood holding a majority of 14,000 votes in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]], leading to Cheung's win being called "one of the stand out upsets of the election".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Manhire |first=Toby |date=14 October 2023 |title=Poised to unseat Michael Wood in Mt Roskill, Carlos Cheung arrives to cheers |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/14-10-2023/poised-to-unseat-michael-wood-in-mt-roskill-carlos-cheung-arrives-to-cheers |access-date=14 October 2023 |website=The Spinoff |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Desmarais |first=Felix |date=16 October 2023 |title=New kids on the forecourt: Who are National's new MPs? |language=en |website=1 News |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/10/16/new-kids-on-the-forecourt-who-are-nationals-new-mps/ |access-date=20 October 2023}}</ref> Cheung is the second Chinese-born MP to hold an electorate seat in New Zealand, the first being [[Pansy Wong]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last=Duoya Lu |date=16 October 2023 |title=Chinese candidates nervous about election fate |language=zh |work=RNZ |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/chinese/500315/chinese-candidates-nervous-about-election-fate |access-date=20 October 2023}}</ref>

==Views and positions==
Cheung stated that he plans to "reduce cost-of-living pressures and restore law and order in our community".<ref name=":2" /> He also planned to reduce costs for landlords.<ref name=":3" />

== Personal life ==
He is married to Fiona Lai, who was elected as a [[Communities and Residents]] candidate to the [[Puketāpapa Local Board]] in 2019 and 2022.<ref name="RESULTS2019">{{cite web |date=18 October 2019 |title=Local board members |url=https://www.aucklandcouncil.govt.nz/elections/electionresults/2019-local-elections-results-mayor-council.pdf |accessdate=21 October 2019 |publisher=Auckland Council}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=15 October 2022 |title=Local elections 2022 – Local board member official results |url=https://www.aucklandcouncil.govt.nz/elections/elections-2022-results/results/local-elections-2022-official-results-local-board-members.pdf |accessdate=15 October 2022 |publisher=Auckland Council}}</ref> They live in Mount Roskill.<ref name=":1" />

